> When the question is what a running system DOES, go and watch it do it — never conclude it from source, and never from a fetch your environment may have blocked

# Observe, do not infer

Reading code tells you what a system is **supposed** to do. It cannot tell you what it **is** doing.
When those two differ — a stale deploy, a tag that loads but never fires, a value transformed three
layers downstream — only observation settles it, and inference produces a confident wrong answer
that is then repeated to the user as fact.

Three times in one session, all on questions that a browser could have answered in a minute:

| Claimed, from reading                                    | Actually                                                         |
| -------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| "`analytics.js` returns an empty file, UA is decommissioned" | The file is ~21.6 KB and served every day. **The sandbox cannot resolve that host.** |
| "The traffic cannot be in GA4 — no GA4 tag is in the page source" | It plainly was, in the user's own screenshot                     |
| "The fix is not deployed — `pointercancel` is not in the bundle" | It was. The response was **gzipped** and the grep ran on compressed bytes |

Each was stated with confidence. Two were written into a public repo's README and a PR before the
user pushed back. The third was caught only because the conclusion was surprising enough to re-check.

## The trap that made two of them identical

**A blocked host and a dead host look exactly the same.** `curl` returning zero bytes, a network
trace with no request, a DNS lookup with no answer — these are what a decommissioned endpoint looks
like, and also what a sandbox with no egress looks like. The failure mode is indistinguishable from
the finding, which is `no-failure-as-valid-value` wearing a different hat.

Before concluding a remote thing is broken, **prove your environment can reach it**:

```bash
dig +short <host>                 # empty answer = your DNS, not their outage
curl -sI <url> | head -1          # no status line at all = you never got there
curl -s --compressed <url> | wc -c  # gzip makes a live file look empty to a naive grep
```

If the host does not resolve, you have measured your sandbox. Say so, and find another instrument —
the Internet Archive's CDX API, a different network path, or the user's own screen.

## What to do instead

| Question                                   | Wrong instrument              | Right instrument                                       |
| ------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
| Does this tag actually send?                | grep the page source          | watch the network for the request, read its parameters |
| Is my fix deployed?                         | reason about merge timing     | fetch the built asset and look; then drive the live UI |
| What does this control really do?           | read the handler              | click it in a browser and measure the result           |
| Is the timing right?                        | read the scheduler            | hook the scheduling call and record the times          |
| Did this redraw?                            | count the deps array          | `MutationObserver` and count the mutations             |

The cost is usually one script. In this session, hooking `AudioBufferSourceNode.start` to record
every scheduled sound took one file and settled four separate timing questions that reading
`subdivDuration` could not — including proving a reported bug was **not** where it appeared to be.

## Say which one you did

When reporting a finding, make the instrument explicit: "measured in the browser", "read from the
source", "inferred from the merge order". A reader can discount an inference; they cannot discount
one they think is a measurement. If you could not observe it, say the conclusion is provisional
rather than rounding it up to fact.

Root cause class: `inferred-not-observed`.
